1
00:00:25,792 --> 00:00:28,125
♪♪

2
00:00:36,370 --> 00:00:38,305
♪♪

3
00:00:49,349 --> 00:00:51,341
♪♪

4
00:01:38,632 --> 00:01:40,610
You'll... You're gonna
be all right! You're all right!

5
00:01:40,634 --> 00:01:41,634
Come on, come on.

6
00:02:05,192 --> 00:02:06,336
Whoa!

7
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:09,305
Whoa. Geez, this
thing weighs a ton.

8
00:02:09,329 --> 00:02:11,608
What's the occasion?

9
00:02:11,632 --> 00:02:13,624
We got something new in.

10
00:02:20,007 --> 00:02:21,498
You call this new?

11
00:02:23,210 --> 00:02:25,688
- Ross.
- Hmm?

12
00:02:25,712 --> 00:02:27,790
Remember those Hawks
tickets I said I might

13
00:02:27,814 --> 00:02:29,305
- be able to get you?
- Yeah.

14
00:02:32,686 --> 00:02:34,430
That's great.

15
00:02:34,454 --> 00:02:36,599
That's really nice. Thank you.

16
00:02:36,623 --> 00:02:39,268
Ah. The new suits
finally came in, huh?

17
00:02:39,292 --> 00:02:41,237
Nice, huh?

18
00:02:41,261 --> 00:02:42,261
Very nice.

19
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:49,779
Reminds me of the suit
they buried my grandfather in.

20
00:02:49,803 --> 00:02:51,714
Sid's all right.

21
00:02:51,738 --> 00:02:54,183
He can't help it if he
lives in a time warp.

22
00:03:03,350 --> 00:03:04,727
- Ben?
- Yeah?

23
00:03:04,751 --> 00:03:05,795
Good to see you.

24
00:03:05,819 --> 00:03:07,697
- Oh, hi, Ross. How you doing?
- Good.

25
00:03:07,721 --> 00:03:08,931
Help you find something?

26
00:03:08,955 --> 00:03:10,266
Well, as a matter of fact,

27
00:03:10,290 --> 00:03:12,201
I was thinking about
buying a new suit.

28
00:03:12,225 --> 00:03:13,469
- No kidding.
- Yeah.

29
00:03:13,493 --> 00:03:14,604
Hey, you talk about timing.

30
00:03:14,628 --> 00:03:15,838
We just got something in.

31
00:03:15,862 --> 00:03:17,558
Here, let me show it to you.

32
00:03:19,566 --> 00:03:21,711
Huh?

33
00:03:21,735 --> 00:03:23,813
Oh, it's you, Ben.

34
00:03:23,837 --> 00:03:25,348
Feel it.

35
00:03:25,372 --> 00:03:28,017
Nothing like the look and feel
of genuine gabardine, is there?

36
00:03:28,041 --> 00:03:30,520
No, no, there isn't.

37
00:03:30,544 --> 00:03:33,322
Oh, th... oh, that's-that's it.

38
00:03:33,346 --> 00:03:34,490
That's it!

39
00:03:34,514 --> 00:03:35,825
Hey, Sid.

40
00:03:35,849 --> 00:03:38,061
I pulled it out as soon
as I heard your voice.

41
00:03:38,085 --> 00:03:39,062
- Oh...
- What do you think?

42
00:03:39,086 --> 00:03:40,830
Oh, I think you got a sale.

43
00:03:40,854 --> 00:03:42,465
I'm sure we have your
measurements on file,

44
00:03:42,489 --> 00:03:44,367
but let me get Enrico
out here just to make sure.

45
00:03:44,391 --> 00:03:46,836
Okay. Uh... Sid?

46
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:48,171
Do you think you could

47
00:03:48,195 --> 00:03:50,061
throw in a couple
of pairs of socks?

48
00:03:52,365 --> 00:03:53,843
Hello, Ben. Ben Matlock.

49
00:03:53,867 --> 00:03:54,844
Hey, Marv.

50
00:03:54,868 --> 00:03:55,878
Hey.

51
00:03:55,902 --> 00:03:56,879
How are you?

52
00:03:56,903 --> 00:03:58,648
I'm fine. What do you think?

53
00:03:58,672 --> 00:04:00,049
- Oh, that's beautiful.
- Yeah.

54
00:04:00,073 --> 00:04:02,085
Beautiful.

55
00:04:02,109 --> 00:04:04,754
You're supposed to be
inventorying shirts, aren't you?

56
00:04:04,778 --> 00:04:05,755
Right away.

57
00:04:05,779 --> 00:04:07,724
Yes.

58
00:04:07,748 --> 00:04:09,659
What's this I hear about
your daughter getting married?

59
00:04:09,683 --> 00:04:10,727
Oh, yeah, this Saturday.

60
00:04:10,751 --> 00:04:11,961
Would you believe?

61
00:04:11,985 --> 00:04:14,130
That's if he doesn't
get cold feet.

62
00:04:14,154 --> 00:04:15,732
That's if my checks
don't bounce.

63
00:04:15,756 --> 00:04:17,200
Either of which
is quite possible.

64
00:04:19,259 --> 00:04:22,138
My oldest, most
favorite customer.

65
00:04:22,162 --> 00:04:23,272
Oh, Enrico.

66
00:04:23,296 --> 00:04:24,307
Enrico will check
your measurements

67
00:04:24,331 --> 00:04:25,675
- and get right to work.
- Oh, good.

68
00:04:25,699 --> 00:04:26,943
How does Monday sound?

69
00:04:26,967 --> 00:04:29,011
Monday? You don't have
to rush on my account.

70
00:04:29,035 --> 00:04:30,246
It's no rush.

71
00:04:30,270 --> 00:04:31,547
No problem.

72
00:04:31,571 --> 00:04:32,548
Please, step up.

73
00:04:32,572 --> 00:04:33,883
Okay. Excuse me.

74
00:04:33,907 --> 00:04:35,952
Yeah, Ben, I'm gonna
leave you with Enrico.

75
00:04:35,976 --> 00:04:38,020
Sid and I have some
business to talk about.

76
00:05:02,969 --> 00:05:04,914
I was just at the bank.

77
00:05:04,938 --> 00:05:06,749
Yeah?

78
00:05:06,773 --> 00:05:08,451
You ask them about the loan?

79
00:05:09,609 --> 00:05:11,687
No, I went in there to
buy a couple of CDs.

80
00:05:11,711 --> 00:05:14,590
Of course I asked
them about the loan.

81
00:05:14,614 --> 00:05:16,526
They turned us down.

82
00:05:16,550 --> 00:05:17,827
We'll just go somewhere else.

83
00:05:17,851 --> 00:05:20,229
We'll just get turned
down somewhere else.

84
00:05:20,253 --> 00:05:22,965
How we gonna make
the payroll next week?

85
00:05:22,989 --> 00:05:24,389
How we gonna pay the rent?

86
00:05:26,426 --> 00:05:28,371
Face it, Sid.

87
00:05:28,395 --> 00:05:30,125
We're not.

88
00:05:35,769 --> 00:05:37,914
So now what?

89
00:05:37,938 --> 00:05:39,201
Bankruptcy?

90
00:05:43,543 --> 00:05:45,488
There's another option.

91
00:05:45,512 --> 00:05:46,589
What?

92
00:05:46,613 --> 00:05:47,590
Well, we have insurance.

93
00:05:47,614 --> 00:05:49,125
The people who own the building,

94
00:05:49,149 --> 00:05:50,126
they have insurance.

95
00:05:50,150 --> 00:05:51,127
So?

96
00:05:51,151 --> 00:05:52,595
So we hire some guy

97
00:05:52,619 --> 00:05:56,199
to torch the place and
we collect the insurance.

98
00:05:56,223 --> 00:05:58,215
We start over again.

99
00:06:02,195 --> 00:06:04,140
- You can't be serious.
- Yeah.

100
00:06:04,164 --> 00:06:05,641
I'm dead serious.

101
00:06:05,665 --> 00:06:08,260
I've given this
a lot of thought.

102
00:06:09,502 --> 00:06:11,747
We'll get caught.

103
00:06:11,771 --> 00:06:13,983
Well, George Clancy
didn't get caught.

104
00:06:14,007 --> 00:06:15,451
But that was an accident.

105
00:06:15,475 --> 00:06:16,485
No, it wasn't.

106
00:06:16,509 --> 00:06:17,486
No accident.

107
00:06:17,510 --> 00:06:18,654
That fire was set.

108
00:06:18,678 --> 00:06:20,857
What if somebody gets hurt?

109
00:06:20,881 --> 00:06:23,726
Nobody was hurt in
George Clancy's fire.

110
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:26,095
My God, Marv.

111
00:06:26,119 --> 00:06:28,631
This is our business.

112
00:06:28,655 --> 00:06:30,867
We started it, we built it.

113
00:06:30,891 --> 00:06:33,502
We put 32 years
of our life into it,

114
00:06:33,526 --> 00:06:35,004
and now you want to destroy it?

115
00:06:35,028 --> 00:06:38,307
Oh... Damn it, will
you open your eyes?

116
00:06:38,331 --> 00:06:39,642
It is destroyed!

117
00:06:39,666 --> 00:06:42,778
It's gone, Sid, it's gone.

118
00:06:42,802 --> 00:06:46,782
We can either let the
creditors pick over the bones,

119
00:06:46,806 --> 00:06:50,486
or we can use
what little we got left

120
00:06:50,510 --> 00:06:52,555
and make it work for us.

121
00:06:52,579 --> 00:06:53,956
George Clancy's
over in the mall.

122
00:06:53,980 --> 00:06:56,792
He's selling more
shoes than he ever did.

123
00:06:56,816 --> 00:06:58,794
It's wrong.

124
00:06:58,818 --> 00:07:02,983
He gave me the name
of the guy he used.

125
00:07:04,457 --> 00:07:06,335
We can talk to him. Hmm?

126
00:07:06,359 --> 00:07:08,938
Talk.

127
00:07:08,962 --> 00:07:10,931
Okay?

128
00:07:16,269 --> 00:07:17,246
He's not gonna show.

129
00:07:17,270 --> 00:07:18,681
Let's get back to the store.

130
00:07:18,705 --> 00:07:19,682
He'll show, he'll show.

131
00:07:19,706 --> 00:07:21,183
Sit down.

132
00:07:21,207 --> 00:07:23,719
Probably casing us right now.

133
00:07:23,743 --> 00:07:25,655
Be sure that we're
not cops or something.

134
00:07:25,679 --> 00:07:27,390
You mean, the police
are looking for this guy?

135
00:07:27,414 --> 00:07:30,026
We're gonna be associating
with a known felon?

136
00:07:30,050 --> 00:07:31,527
Sid, will you relax?

137
00:07:31,551 --> 00:07:32,561
Relax?

138
00:07:32,585 --> 00:07:34,563
What if the police
are casing him?

139
00:07:34,587 --> 00:07:35,987
Mr. Shea?

140
00:07:37,624 --> 00:07:39,702
Yeah. I'm Marv Shea.

141
00:07:39,726 --> 00:07:41,637
Al Brown.

142
00:07:41,661 --> 00:07:42,972
You mind if I sit down?

143
00:07:42,996 --> 00:07:43,996
No, sit down.

144
00:07:45,999 --> 00:07:47,476
Sid Franklin, my partner.

145
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:49,312
Mr. Franklin, nice to meet you.

146
00:07:49,336 --> 00:07:52,148
You know, you fellas have
got a very nice little store.

147
00:07:52,172 --> 00:07:55,117
Two stories, nice big
window out in front.

148
00:07:55,141 --> 00:07:56,452
Two more in the rear.

149
00:07:56,476 --> 00:07:59,188
Couple of rooms in the back.

150
00:07:59,212 --> 00:08:01,891
Incidentally, how many are
there in the back, two or three?

151
00:08:01,915 --> 00:08:03,159
That was difficult to tell.

152
00:08:03,183 --> 00:08:05,094
Three. Three back there.

153
00:08:05,118 --> 00:08:07,697
There's the office and
a sewing room and-and

154
00:08:07,721 --> 00:08:09,265
a bathroom.

155
00:08:09,289 --> 00:08:10,967
Ah, that's good, that's good.

156
00:08:10,991 --> 00:08:12,802
You-You got a gas furnace?

157
00:08:12,826 --> 00:08:14,937
Downstairs.

158
00:08:14,961 --> 00:08:16,138
You were in the store?

159
00:08:16,162 --> 00:08:17,406
This morning.

160
00:08:17,430 --> 00:08:20,195
I was there from 8:30
on; I didn't see you.

161
00:08:21,935 --> 00:08:24,837
Mr. Franklin, I didn't
want you to see me.

162
00:08:28,041 --> 00:08:30,419
Now, when would
you like it to be done?

163
00:08:30,443 --> 00:08:32,321
This Saturday night.

164
00:08:32,345 --> 00:08:33,756
Your daughter's getting
married this Saturday night.

165
00:08:33,780 --> 00:08:35,591
Yeah, I know. That's
why, that's why.

166
00:08:35,615 --> 00:08:36,659
Store'll be empty.

167
00:08:36,683 --> 00:08:38,049
Everybody will
be at the reception.

168
00:08:40,086 --> 00:08:41,452
How much?

169
00:08:42,455 --> 00:08:43,432
$40,000.

170
00:08:43,456 --> 00:08:46,902
15 before, 20 after
and five right now.

171
00:08:46,926 --> 00:08:48,726
That's the earnest money
that we talked about.

172
00:08:49,662 --> 00:08:51,207
Wait a minute.

173
00:08:51,231 --> 00:08:53,075
You didn't say anything
about giving him money.

174
00:08:53,099 --> 00:08:56,846
Mr. Shea, Mr. Franklin,
thank you very much.

175
00:08:56,870 --> 00:08:58,065
I will be in touch.

176
00:09:14,187 --> 00:09:15,164
No, that's fine.

177
00:09:15,188 --> 00:09:16,532
No, I understand.

178
00:09:16,556 --> 00:09:18,434
I understand. That's...

179
00:09:18,458 --> 00:09:21,137
I thought it over, Marv.

180
00:09:21,161 --> 00:09:22,805
Yeah, what?

181
00:09:22,829 --> 00:09:24,140
Yeah, 9:30. I'll be waiting.

182
00:09:24,164 --> 00:09:25,474
I can't let you do this.

183
00:09:27,100 --> 00:09:30,279
We built every inch of this
business honestly and fairly.

184
00:09:30,303 --> 00:09:31,947
And I'd just as soon
lose it honestly and fairly

185
00:09:31,971 --> 00:09:33,115
than see it burned to the ground

186
00:09:33,139 --> 00:09:34,316
- Sid...
- by some criminal!

187
00:09:34,340 --> 00:09:35,384
Sid, it is done!

188
00:09:35,408 --> 00:09:36,819
That was him on the phone.

189
00:09:36,843 --> 00:09:37,820
Now, listen.

190
00:09:37,844 --> 00:09:38,988
Saturday night,

191
00:09:39,012 --> 00:09:40,856
9:30, I am going to
leave the reception.

192
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:42,925
I'm going to meet him in the
alley right behind the ballroom.

193
00:09:42,949 --> 00:09:44,660
I'm going to give
him some money.

194
00:09:44,684 --> 00:09:45,961
- No.
- He's going to

195
00:09:45,985 --> 00:09:48,464
come back here and he's
going to burn this place down,

196
00:09:48,488 --> 00:09:51,367
and we are going to
have something to show

197
00:09:51,391 --> 00:09:53,035
for 32 years of sweat.

198
00:09:53,059 --> 00:09:54,059
No!

199
00:09:55,595 --> 00:10:00,042
Sid... we declare bankruptcy,

200
00:10:00,066 --> 00:10:02,011
we are never going
to get out from under it.

201
00:10:02,035 --> 00:10:03,312
You understand that?

202
00:10:03,336 --> 00:10:06,048
We are too damn old!

203
00:10:06,072 --> 00:10:08,350
Now, I'm not going to
spend the rest of my life

204
00:10:08,374 --> 00:10:10,419
living off of the government.

205
00:10:10,443 --> 00:10:13,607
Or what's worse,
living off of my kids.

206
00:10:15,348 --> 00:10:18,761
Sid, collecting
on the insurance,

207
00:10:18,785 --> 00:10:22,098
that's the only way out for us.

208
00:10:22,122 --> 00:10:24,934
God help us.

209
00:10:24,958 --> 00:10:26,927
God help us, Marv.

210
00:10:51,951 --> 00:10:53,462
Where's your date?

211
00:10:53,486 --> 00:10:55,478
Didn't bring one.

212
00:11:04,631 --> 00:11:07,610
Have you thought about it?

213
00:11:07,634 --> 00:11:10,412
Are you still planning to do it?

214
00:11:10,436 --> 00:11:11,995
Of course.

215
00:11:13,540 --> 00:11:15,217
Marv, think.

216
00:11:15,241 --> 00:11:16,886
Think about her.

217
00:11:16,910 --> 00:11:18,787
If something went
wrong and we got caught

218
00:11:18,811 --> 00:11:22,491
and sent to prison, it
would kill your daughter.

219
00:11:22,515 --> 00:11:25,995
You just can't risk it, Marv.

220
00:11:26,019 --> 00:11:28,998
Why don't we meet with
Mr. Brown and tell him the deal is off?

221
00:11:29,022 --> 00:11:30,866
You nuts or what?

222
00:11:30,890 --> 00:11:32,401
Please, Marv.

223
00:11:32,425 --> 00:11:33,836
Try to get the 5,000 back.

224
00:11:33,860 --> 00:11:36,038
Look, I don't know what
we'll do about the store,

225
00:11:36,062 --> 00:11:38,474
but we just can't go to jail.

226
00:11:38,498 --> 00:11:40,490
I'll go with you, if you like.

227
00:11:46,706 --> 00:11:48,698
Forget it.

228
00:12:28,514 --> 00:12:29,812
Marv?

229
00:12:36,089 --> 00:12:37,433
Oh!

230
00:12:37,457 --> 00:12:41,003
Oh! Oh, Marv!

231
00:12:41,027 --> 00:12:42,771
Hey, what's going on back here?!

232
00:12:42,795 --> 00:12:45,241
Huh?

233
00:12:45,265 --> 00:12:46,408
Don't move, man!

234
00:12:46,432 --> 00:12:47,593
Hold it right there!

235
00:12:52,105 --> 00:12:54,116
<i>Well, I didn't like the idea of</i>

236
00:12:54,140 --> 00:12:57,453
Marv being with some kind
of a fugitive all by himself,

237
00:12:57,477 --> 00:13:00,322
so I followed him.

238
00:13:00,346 --> 00:13:02,524
I heard something, and...

239
00:13:02,548 --> 00:13:04,893
went to take a look,
and there he was.

240
00:13:04,917 --> 00:13:07,263
Oh.

241
00:13:07,287 --> 00:13:10,499
Did you see anybody
else in the alley at all?

242
00:13:10,523 --> 00:13:12,034
No.

243
00:13:12,058 --> 00:13:13,969
But it had to be
the arsonist, Ben.

244
00:13:13,993 --> 00:13:17,773
Marv had $15,000 on him.

245
00:13:17,797 --> 00:13:20,776
Last thing I said to
him was, "Think about it.

246
00:13:20,800 --> 00:13:22,792
It's not too late."

247
00:13:24,337 --> 00:13:26,248
You know, maybe he
did change his mind

248
00:13:26,272 --> 00:13:27,750
and put up a fuss,

249
00:13:27,774 --> 00:13:30,352
and then the guy stabbed
him, grabbed the money,

250
00:13:30,376 --> 00:13:32,755
and he took off when
he heard me coming.

251
00:13:32,779 --> 00:13:34,623
What'd you say
this fellow's name is?

252
00:13:34,647 --> 00:13:36,892
Al Brown.

253
00:13:36,916 --> 00:13:39,495
George Clancy recommended him.

254
00:13:39,519 --> 00:13:42,231
He was the guy that burned
his old shoe store down.

255
00:13:42,255 --> 00:13:43,832
Mmm.

256
00:13:43,856 --> 00:13:46,435
Marv and I were partners,
Ben, and we didn't always agree,

257
00:13:46,459 --> 00:13:49,805
and sometimes we
fought over things, but...

258
00:13:51,331 --> 00:13:52,708
He was real cheap, you know?

259
00:13:52,732 --> 00:13:55,964
Oh, I know he was.

260
00:13:57,503 --> 00:14:00,598
But... why would I kill him?

261
00:14:04,711 --> 00:14:08,590
Enrico overheard that
argument between the two of you

262
00:14:08,614 --> 00:14:11,427
the other night in the office.

263
00:14:11,451 --> 00:14:14,296
And he told the D.A. that

264
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:18,167
you were very, very
mad at Marv... I was.

265
00:14:18,191 --> 00:14:23,906
I swear to you,
Ben... I didn't kill him.

266
00:14:23,930 --> 00:14:25,741
I know.

267
00:14:25,765 --> 00:14:27,165
I believe you.

268
00:14:29,369 --> 00:14:34,583
I've been a satisfied
customer of yours for 20 years.

269
00:14:34,607 --> 00:14:37,600
And I'm gonna do
my very best for you.

270
00:14:47,954 --> 00:14:49,531
<i>Al Brown?</i>

271
00:14:49,555 --> 00:14:51,567
Probably an alias.

272
00:14:51,591 --> 00:14:53,168
How am I supposed to find him?

273
00:14:53,192 --> 00:14:56,305
Same way Marv Shea
did; through George Clancy.

274
00:14:56,329 --> 00:14:58,507
Mr. Matlock, you haven't
touched your eggs.

275
00:14:58,531 --> 00:15:01,365
Uh, no, ma'am, I'm just
having coffee this morning.

276
00:15:03,436 --> 00:15:08,917
George Clancy has a
brand-new shoe store in the mall.

277
00:15:08,941 --> 00:15:12,521
The old shoe store burned down.

278
00:15:12,545 --> 00:15:14,289
MRS. McCARDLE:
Anything wrong with 'em?

279
00:15:14,313 --> 00:15:17,359
With...? Oh, oh...
no, oh, no, no!

280
00:15:17,383 --> 00:15:19,261
I'm just kinda,
kinda watching it.

281
00:15:21,287 --> 00:15:22,865
Your client ever see
this Al Brown guy?

282
00:15:22,889 --> 00:15:25,968
Yeah, he's, uh, medium
height, late 40s, grayish hair.

283
00:15:25,992 --> 00:15:27,035
That's about it.

284
00:15:27,059 --> 00:15:28,237
He's got an accomplice,

285
00:15:28,261 --> 00:15:30,839
but, uh, Sid didn't
get a good look at him.

286
00:15:30,863 --> 00:15:32,841
I've always cooked my
scrambled eggs that way.

287
00:15:32,865 --> 00:15:37,012
Nice and fluffy and a
bit on the loose side.

288
00:15:37,036 --> 00:15:39,181
M-Mrs. McCardle,

289
00:15:39,205 --> 00:15:41,884
I'm-I'm sure your eggs
are, are, are wonderful,

290
00:15:41,908 --> 00:15:44,953
but the fact is
I-I had a fitting

291
00:15:44,977 --> 00:15:46,622
for a new suit the other day

292
00:15:46,646 --> 00:15:48,857
and it was a
little, a little tight.

293
00:15:48,881 --> 00:15:51,059
It's not, not that I've gained.

294
00:15:51,083 --> 00:15:53,762
My late husband
William loved my eggs.

295
00:15:53,786 --> 00:15:55,998
"Mary Katherine," he'd say,

296
00:15:56,022 --> 00:15:57,433
"you've done it again."

297
00:15:57,457 --> 00:15:59,668
He was a darlin' man, darlin'.

298
00:15:59,692 --> 00:16:00,869
Darlin', yeah.

299
00:16:00,893 --> 00:16:03,272
Just-Just for a few
days, and-and then...

300
00:16:03,296 --> 00:16:05,707
It was a pleasure
cooking for a man like that.

301
00:16:05,731 --> 00:16:09,077
Ah, he could stuff his face.

302
00:16:09,101 --> 00:16:11,346
Well, m-maybe on the
weekend we could...

303
00:16:11,370 --> 00:16:14,082
I'll bet that if he
was here right now,

304
00:16:14,106 --> 00:16:15,717
he'd know what to
do with those eggs.

305
00:16:15,741 --> 00:16:17,586
He'd dig in.

306
00:16:17,610 --> 00:16:20,155
Mrs. McCardle?

307
00:16:20,179 --> 00:16:23,392
Mrs. McCardle, look.

308
00:16:23,416 --> 00:16:25,527
Look, Mrs. McCardle.

309
00:16:25,551 --> 00:16:26,728
Mmm!

310
00:16:26,752 --> 00:16:29,331
Good eggs!

311
00:16:29,355 --> 00:16:32,325
Mmm-mmm!

312
00:16:35,261 --> 00:16:37,539
Oh, that's so beautiful.

313
00:16:37,563 --> 00:16:39,641
Well, perhaps since you're
here, you should try it on.

314
00:16:39,665 --> 00:16:42,744
Oh, that's a good idea...
Here, here, here, here.

315
00:16:42,768 --> 00:16:43,812
All right.

316
00:16:43,836 --> 00:16:45,147
Just give me that
beauty. Ha-ha-ha.

317
00:16:45,171 --> 00:16:46,682
Uh-huh.

318
00:16:46,706 --> 00:16:48,484
Ah-ha-ha-ha-ha.

319
00:16:48,508 --> 00:16:52,821
You've been working at this
store as long as I can remember.

320
00:16:52,845 --> 00:16:55,858
Oh, uh, since the
first day it opened.

321
00:16:55,882 --> 00:16:59,728
Yeah. It must have
been quite a shock

322
00:16:59,752 --> 00:17:04,433
when you heard Marv say
he was gonna burn it down.

323
00:17:04,457 --> 00:17:06,001
It was an outrage.

324
00:17:06,025 --> 00:17:07,803
It made me furious.

325
00:17:07,827 --> 00:17:09,972
It made all of us furious.

326
00:17:09,996 --> 00:17:12,040
What do you mean, "all of us"?

327
00:17:12,064 --> 00:17:16,912
All of us: me,
Sid, Ross, Cheryl.

328
00:17:16,936 --> 00:17:18,847
How did Ross and
Cheryl find out about it?

329
00:17:18,871 --> 00:17:20,782
I told them.

330
00:17:20,806 --> 00:17:23,519
How could I keep something
so horrible to myself?

331
00:17:23,543 --> 00:17:25,521
Ah, how does it feel?

332
00:17:25,545 --> 00:17:27,022
Perfect.

333
00:17:27,046 --> 00:17:28,257
Uh-huh.

334
00:17:28,281 --> 00:17:31,193
Maybe just a little
tight right through there.

335
00:17:31,217 --> 00:17:33,395
Oh? A little tight?

336
00:17:33,419 --> 00:17:35,597
It's a miracle you can breathe.

337
00:17:35,621 --> 00:17:37,432
Have you gained weight?

338
00:17:37,456 --> 00:17:39,501
No!

339
00:17:39,525 --> 00:17:41,303
Gained weight!

340
00:17:41,327 --> 00:17:42,371
I'm very careful.

341
00:17:42,395 --> 00:17:43,405
Look at that.

342
00:17:43,429 --> 00:17:45,841
You sure this is the same cut?

343
00:17:45,865 --> 00:17:47,509
There's only one cut.

344
00:17:47,533 --> 00:17:50,679
Go right back in there
and take them off.

345
00:17:50,703 --> 00:17:52,481
Enrico will fix.

346
00:17:52,505 --> 00:17:53,815
Gained weight.

347
00:17:53,839 --> 00:17:55,584
Your sewing machine
probably zigged

348
00:17:55,608 --> 00:17:58,053
when it was supposed to zag.

349
00:18:05,318 --> 00:18:07,296
Those in the brown, but...

350
00:18:07,320 --> 00:18:09,983
Well, I brought
a brown pair out.

351
00:18:11,624 --> 00:18:16,471
Hi. Uh, my salesman said
you wanted to talk to me.

352
00:18:16,495 --> 00:18:18,740
George Clancy?

353
00:18:18,764 --> 00:18:20,809
That's right.

354
00:18:20,833 --> 00:18:23,211
I like this place better
than your old place.

355
00:18:23,235 --> 00:18:24,613
Oh, thank you.

356
00:18:24,637 --> 00:18:27,649
Must have collected a lot of
money on your fire insurance

357
00:18:27,673 --> 00:18:29,335
to pay for this
kind of lease, huh?

358
00:18:31,777 --> 00:18:33,288
What do you want?

359
00:18:33,312 --> 00:18:35,223
Name is Conrad McMasters.

360
00:18:35,247 --> 00:18:39,728
I have a friend who has a
little restaurant downtown.

361
00:18:39,752 --> 00:18:42,664
Losing his shirt, so
he's interested in doing

362
00:18:42,688 --> 00:18:45,000
to his place what you
did to your old place.

363
00:18:45,024 --> 00:18:48,637
Hey, this is a shoe store.

364
00:18:48,661 --> 00:18:50,706
You want shoes, I can help you.

365
00:18:50,730 --> 00:18:52,608
You want something
else, I can't help you.

366
00:18:52,632 --> 00:18:55,877
Okay. You got this in brown?

367
00:18:55,901 --> 00:18:57,579
Brown?

368
00:18:57,603 --> 00:19:01,267
Yeah, brown, as in Al Brown?

369
00:19:07,046 --> 00:19:08,790
Who'd you say you were?

370
00:19:08,814 --> 00:19:11,226
Just somebody who's looking

371
00:19:11,250 --> 00:19:14,277
to retain his
services, that's all.

372
00:19:15,788 --> 00:19:18,166
Well...

373
00:19:18,190 --> 00:19:20,502
Ben! Hey, you got
the new suit on, huh?

374
00:19:20,526 --> 00:19:21,503
Looks great.

375
00:19:21,527 --> 00:19:24,339
Uh, well, actually,
this is my old suit.

376
00:19:24,363 --> 00:19:25,974
Enrico's still working
on the new one.

377
00:19:25,998 --> 00:19:26,975
Oh.

378
00:19:26,999 --> 00:19:28,310
Yeah, yeah.

379
00:19:28,334 --> 00:19:30,312
Listen, Ben... Mm-hmm?

380
00:19:30,336 --> 00:19:31,346
How's Sid doing?

381
00:19:31,370 --> 00:19:32,648
Pretty good.

382
00:19:32,672 --> 00:19:34,049
He's out on bail.

383
00:19:34,073 --> 00:19:35,751
I sent him home
to get some rest.

384
00:19:35,775 --> 00:19:38,086
I can't believe anybody would
think he committed murder.

385
00:19:38,110 --> 00:19:39,087
Yeah.

386
00:19:39,111 --> 00:19:41,857
- He's the most decent guy I know.
- Hmm...

387
00:19:41,881 --> 00:19:44,726
I hear you knew that
Marv was gonna go talk

388
00:19:44,750 --> 00:19:47,029
to the arsonist that night.

389
00:19:47,053 --> 00:19:49,464
Yeah, I knew.

390
00:19:49,488 --> 00:19:54,069
Did you happen to notice
if anybody left when he did?

391
00:19:54,093 --> 00:19:55,771
I didn't even see <i>him</i> leave.

392
00:19:55,795 --> 00:19:57,806
Next thing you know,
the police showed up.

393
00:19:57,830 --> 00:20:00,509
I thought they were gonna
say the store had burned down,

394
00:20:00,533 --> 00:20:02,177
not that Marv had been murdered.

395
00:20:02,201 --> 00:20:03,779
Oh.

396
00:20:03,803 --> 00:20:06,548
Tell me the truth, Ben.

397
00:20:06,572 --> 00:20:08,803
Are you gonna be
able to get Sid off?

398
00:20:10,576 --> 00:20:12,374
I'm gonna give him my best shot.

399
00:20:18,751 --> 00:20:19,878
You Conrad?

400
00:20:23,189 --> 00:20:24,599
Yeah. Who are you?

401
00:20:24,623 --> 00:20:27,369
Al Brown.

402
00:20:27,393 --> 00:20:29,137
You're not Al Brown.

403
00:20:29,161 --> 00:20:30,272
How do you know?

404
00:20:30,296 --> 00:20:34,376
I know what Al Brown looks like.

405
00:20:34,400 --> 00:20:35,744
AI's a busy man.

406
00:20:35,768 --> 00:20:37,112
I do his research.

407
00:20:37,136 --> 00:20:40,982
And if you don't like
it, hey, it's been real.

408
00:20:41,006 --> 00:20:43,134
Wait! Hey!

409
00:20:46,112 --> 00:20:50,277
All right... the restaurant's
at 1715 Hightower.

410
00:20:53,085 --> 00:20:55,297
It's called the
Rio Bar and Grill.

411
00:20:55,321 --> 00:20:57,916
My friend wants it done
before the end of the week.

412
00:20:59,759 --> 00:21:02,070
I'll look it over
tonight, talk to Al

413
00:21:02,094 --> 00:21:04,106
and get back to
you with an estimate.

414
00:21:04,130 --> 00:21:05,674
Is that okay by you?

415
00:21:05,698 --> 00:21:08,009
No money till I talk to the man.

416
00:21:08,033 --> 00:21:12,129
He wouldn't have
it any other way.

417
00:21:59,118 --> 00:22:02,364
Uh, hey, Cheryl, I-I've
been looking for you.

418
00:22:02,388 --> 00:22:03,665
I'm missing an invoice.

419
00:22:03,689 --> 00:22:05,233
I can't find it.

420
00:22:05,257 --> 00:22:08,170
Oh... oh, have-have-have
you got a few minutes to talk?

421
00:22:08,194 --> 00:22:10,405
As a matter of fact, I don't.

422
00:22:10,429 --> 00:22:12,274
I told Marv's wife I'd stop by

423
00:22:12,298 --> 00:22:13,708
and help her with the
funeral arrangements.

424
00:22:13,732 --> 00:22:15,043
T-Tomorrow, maybe?

425
00:22:15,067 --> 00:22:16,044
Uh, sure.

426
00:22:16,068 --> 00:22:19,214
Yeah. Tomorrow, maybe...

427
00:22:19,238 --> 00:22:23,071
Tomorrow, maybe...
Tomorrow, maybe...

428
00:24:02,975 --> 00:24:05,968
♪♪

429
00:24:39,044 --> 00:24:40,044
Hey!

430
00:25:22,454 --> 00:25:24,446
Oh...

431
00:25:53,886 --> 00:25:56,197
Can you describe the
man you were following?

432
00:25:56,221 --> 00:25:59,067
Yeah, uh... 5'9",

433
00:25:59,091 --> 00:26:02,737
thin, red hair, wearing
a blue jogging suit.

434
00:26:02,761 --> 00:26:05,540
Conrad, are you okay?

435
00:26:05,564 --> 00:26:08,710
Yeah, I'm fine. They were
just trying to scare me, I think.

436
00:26:08,734 --> 00:26:11,179
Doors were locked
from the outside.

437
00:26:11,203 --> 00:26:12,614
They were trying to kill you.

438
00:26:12,638 --> 00:26:14,582
Who's "they"?

439
00:26:14,606 --> 00:26:17,485
The arsonist who
probably killed Marvin Shea.

440
00:26:17,509 --> 00:26:19,421
You feel like looking
at some mug shots?

441
00:26:19,445 --> 00:26:20,922
Damn right.

442
00:26:20,946 --> 00:26:22,938
I want to get these guys.

443
00:26:32,157 --> 00:26:33,435
Oh, no. Ross!

444
00:26:33,459 --> 00:26:34,903
- Oh, I got it. I got it.
- Oh! Thanks.

445
00:26:34,927 --> 00:26:36,237
- I got it. I got it, I...
- Thanks.

446
00:26:36,261 --> 00:26:38,306
Guess I should
have made two trips.

447
00:26:38,330 --> 00:26:40,008
Ross isn't here.

448
00:26:40,032 --> 00:26:42,811
Sid gave him the day off.

449
00:26:42,835 --> 00:26:45,647
I just stopped by to
pick up my new suit.

450
00:26:45,671 --> 00:26:47,449
Oh, well, you want
me to get it for you?

451
00:26:47,473 --> 00:26:50,819
En-Enrico already
did. I-I'm wearing it.

452
00:26:50,843 --> 00:26:53,254
Oh! Well, it looks nice.

453
00:26:53,278 --> 00:26:55,090
Thanks.

454
00:26:55,114 --> 00:26:56,157
Thinning.

455
00:26:56,181 --> 00:26:59,661
Well... Oh, I was, um...

456
00:26:59,685 --> 00:27:03,565
I was, um, poking around in-in
Marv's office the other day...

457
00:27:03,589 --> 00:27:05,600
Uh, yesterday, after you left...

458
00:27:05,624 --> 00:27:10,371
And-and I found this in...
way back in one of his drawers.

459
00:27:10,395 --> 00:27:11,773
Why would Marv have a clipping

460
00:27:11,797 --> 00:27:14,142
from a hit-and-run
three years ago?

461
00:27:14,166 --> 00:27:16,311
I was gonna ask you.

462
00:27:16,335 --> 00:27:17,812
- I have no idea.
- Oh.

463
00:27:17,836 --> 00:27:19,447
Did you ask Sid?

464
00:27:19,471 --> 00:27:21,030
He didn't know, either.

465
00:27:22,040 --> 00:27:27,655
Huh... So, um... you were, uh...

466
00:27:27,679 --> 00:27:29,891
you were at the reception
the other night, huh?

467
00:27:29,915 --> 00:27:30,892
You bet.

468
00:27:30,916 --> 00:27:34,262
Anything I can get off that
man free and not work 14 hours?

469
00:27:34,286 --> 00:27:35,396
I was for that.

470
00:27:35,420 --> 00:27:36,631
Yeah.

471
00:27:36,655 --> 00:27:37,832
Marv was cheap, all right.

472
00:27:37,856 --> 00:27:39,000
Yeah.

473
00:27:39,024 --> 00:27:40,568
So you were there.

474
00:27:40,592 --> 00:27:42,003
Along with 100 other people,

475
00:27:42,027 --> 00:27:43,605
stashing it away, just like me.

476
00:27:44,963 --> 00:27:48,009
Did, uh, did you stay in
the ballroom the whole time?

477
00:27:48,033 --> 00:27:50,245
Yes.

478
00:27:50,269 --> 00:27:52,147
With your husband?

479
00:27:52,171 --> 00:27:55,183
Well, I hung out
with a lot of people.

480
00:27:55,207 --> 00:27:59,369
With Enrico and
his family, and Ross.

481
00:28:01,079 --> 00:28:02,223
Come to think of it,

482
00:28:02,247 --> 00:28:04,058
he wasn't there the whole night.

483
00:28:04,082 --> 00:28:05,527
Who?

484
00:28:05,551 --> 00:28:06,694
Ross.

485
00:28:06,718 --> 00:28:07,862
I wanted to introduce him

486
00:28:07,886 --> 00:28:09,130
to this girl that
I was talking to,

487
00:28:09,154 --> 00:28:11,299
but I couldn't find him.

488
00:28:11,323 --> 00:28:13,792
Seems it was right around 9:30.

489
00:28:22,367 --> 00:28:23,511
Well, hi, Ben.

490
00:28:23,535 --> 00:28:25,880
Ross. Can I talk
to you for a minute?

491
00:28:25,904 --> 00:28:27,715
Sure. Yeah. Come
on in and sit down.

492
00:28:27,739 --> 00:28:28,950
Okay. Okay.

493
00:28:28,974 --> 00:28:30,118
Can I get you
something to drink?

494
00:28:30,142 --> 00:28:31,386
- Nah.
- It'll have to be something soft.

495
00:28:31,410 --> 00:28:32,954
I've been on the wagon
for a couple of years.

496
00:28:32,978 --> 00:28:34,189
That's right.

497
00:28:34,213 --> 00:28:36,257
You used to put it away
pretty good, didn't you?

498
00:28:36,281 --> 00:28:38,193
Oh, yeah. But that's over.

499
00:28:38,217 --> 00:28:40,662
What, did you just quit?

500
00:28:40,686 --> 00:28:42,063
Yeah. It wasn't hard.

501
00:28:42,087 --> 00:28:43,087
Huh.

502
00:28:45,257 --> 00:28:46,748
So...

503
00:28:47,960 --> 00:28:49,938
You lied to me.

504
00:28:49,962 --> 00:28:51,606
Excuse me?

505
00:28:51,630 --> 00:28:54,142
You left the
reception that night.

506
00:28:54,166 --> 00:28:55,600
How come?

507
00:29:01,607 --> 00:29:03,484
Mm-hmm. All right.

508
00:29:03,508 --> 00:29:04,986
Mr. Matlock?

509
00:29:05,010 --> 00:29:06,020
Mr. Matlock...

510
00:29:06,044 --> 00:29:07,822
Mrs. McCardle, what
are you doing here?

511
00:29:07,846 --> 00:29:10,458
Your lunch. You went
off without breakfast,

512
00:29:10,482 --> 00:29:12,327
and you've got to have
something if you're going

513
00:29:12,351 --> 00:29:14,562
to be arguing with judges
and killers and the like.

514
00:29:14,586 --> 00:29:17,065
Now, why don't you sit right
down here on this bench?

515
00:29:17,089 --> 00:29:21,569
Now, this was my late
husband's favorite lunch.

516
00:29:21,593 --> 00:29:24,138
A meat loaf sandwich
with a slice of onion

517
00:29:24,162 --> 00:29:25,974
and a garlic pickle.

518
00:29:25,998 --> 00:29:28,810
He'd say, "Mary Katherine,
you've done it again."

519
00:29:28,834 --> 00:29:29,978
Oh, yeah.

520
00:29:30,002 --> 00:29:32,647
Well, I've worlds of
work to do, so enjoy.

521
00:29:32,671 --> 00:29:33,781
Ta-ta!

522
00:29:33,805 --> 00:29:34,916
His lunch.

523
00:29:34,940 --> 00:29:36,351
It's a meat loaf sandwich

524
00:29:36,375 --> 00:29:38,753
with a slice of onion
and a garlic pickle.

525
00:29:38,777 --> 00:29:40,769
Well, good-bye.

526
00:29:49,388 --> 00:29:50,598
<i>Mr. Gordon,</i>

527
00:29:50,622 --> 00:29:54,102
would you please tell
the court what you saw

528
00:29:54,126 --> 00:29:56,337
the night Marvin
Shea was murdered?

529
00:29:56,361 --> 00:29:58,606
Well, I was at his
daughter's wedding reception,

530
00:29:58,630 --> 00:30:01,175
along with him and
all the other guests,

531
00:30:01,199 --> 00:30:04,646
when, around 9:30, I saw him
slip out one of the side doors,

532
00:30:04,670 --> 00:30:05,813
so I followed him.

533
00:30:05,837 --> 00:30:07,015
Why did you follow him?

534
00:30:07,039 --> 00:30:09,250
Well, because Enrico,
the tailor at the store,

535
00:30:09,274 --> 00:30:12,220
had told me that Marv planned
to leave the party and meet with

536
00:30:12,244 --> 00:30:14,122
the arsonist who was
going to burn down the store.

537
00:30:14,146 --> 00:30:17,158
Well, I went after him because
I wanted to stop him somehow.

538
00:30:17,182 --> 00:30:19,327
Make him see that what
he was doing was wrong.

539
00:30:19,351 --> 00:30:20,795
Yeah.

540
00:30:20,819 --> 00:30:22,263
Well, did you stop him?

541
00:30:22,287 --> 00:30:23,498
No.

542
00:30:23,522 --> 00:30:24,866
By the time I got to the alley,

543
00:30:24,890 --> 00:30:26,968
he was already
talking to the guy.

544
00:30:28,627 --> 00:30:30,255
So I just hung back and watched.

545
00:30:31,863 --> 00:30:33,608
I saw him hand the
guy an envelope.

546
00:30:33,632 --> 00:30:36,978
A-And then it sounded like he
was demanding more money.

547
00:30:37,002 --> 00:30:38,212
The next thing I knew,

548
00:30:38,236 --> 00:30:39,681
the other guy had
a knife in his hand,

549
00:30:39,705 --> 00:30:40,985
and Marv was
struggling with him.

550
00:30:44,643 --> 00:30:50,258
Finally... the guy
raised his hand

551
00:30:50,282 --> 00:30:52,114
and brought it down
in Marv's chest.

552
00:30:53,452 --> 00:30:57,565
Marv went down...
and the guy took off.

553
00:30:57,589 --> 00:30:59,717
What'd this man look like?

554
00:31:00,759 --> 00:31:03,471
Medium height... longish hair...

555
00:31:03,495 --> 00:31:05,173
That's about all I could see.

556
00:31:05,197 --> 00:31:06,941
Well, I-I didn't get a
good look at his face.

557
00:31:06,965 --> 00:31:08,092
It was too dark.

558
00:31:09,968 --> 00:31:11,312
Sure it wasn't the defendant?

559
00:31:11,336 --> 00:31:12,880
Positive.

560
00:31:12,904 --> 00:31:14,549
You said it was dark.

561
00:31:14,573 --> 00:31:15,917
Well, you don't have
to see someone's face

562
00:31:15,941 --> 00:31:17,485
to know who they are.

563
00:31:17,509 --> 00:31:19,721
Well, you can
tell by their build...

564
00:31:19,745 --> 00:31:22,757
the way they
move, their clothes.

565
00:31:22,781 --> 00:31:25,026
Now, I've worked for
Sid Franklin for five years.

566
00:31:25,050 --> 00:31:28,529
And the man that I saw in
that alley that night was not him.

567
00:31:28,553 --> 00:31:31,682
I am absolutely, 100% positive.

568
00:31:32,691 --> 00:31:37,372
Uh, Mr. Gordon,
why did you wait?

569
00:31:37,396 --> 00:31:39,407
Why didn't you come forward

570
00:31:39,431 --> 00:31:42,367
with, uh, your story right away?

571
00:31:44,603 --> 00:31:48,316
Because I figured if the man
that I saw killed Marvin Shea,

572
00:31:48,340 --> 00:31:51,139
he'd have no qualms
about killing me.

573
00:31:55,147 --> 00:31:57,692
I'm not much of a hero.

574
00:31:57,716 --> 00:31:59,651
I was afraid.

575
00:32:01,386 --> 00:32:02,445
Yeah.

576
00:32:03,455 --> 00:32:04,799
Thank you, Mr. Gordon.

577
00:32:04,823 --> 00:32:06,621
Mr. Burgess.

578
00:32:08,693 --> 00:32:10,638
No questions, Your Honor.

579
00:32:10,662 --> 00:32:14,064
Witness may step down.

580
00:32:16,268 --> 00:32:18,169
So, where are we now?

581
00:32:20,238 --> 00:32:23,868
Well, if we can find this Al
Brown, we're in business.

582
00:32:32,584 --> 00:32:33,861
Hello, Petey.

583
00:32:33,885 --> 00:32:35,663
What's up?

584
00:32:35,687 --> 00:32:38,699
You know that guy that wanted
you to torch that restaurant?

585
00:32:38,723 --> 00:32:41,068
The guy who didn't check out?

586
00:32:41,092 --> 00:32:42,603
The guy you took care of.

587
00:32:42,627 --> 00:32:44,272
Yeah.

588
00:32:44,296 --> 00:32:46,541
Well, I was wrong.

589
00:32:46,565 --> 00:32:47,542
I'm really sorry.

590
00:32:47,566 --> 00:32:48,643
Al!

591
00:32:48,667 --> 00:32:50,244
It's so nice to
finally meet you.

592
00:32:50,268 --> 00:32:52,814
Pete has told me
so much about you.

593
00:32:52,838 --> 00:32:56,832
He's also told Lieutenant
Lasky over there...

594
00:32:58,043 --> 00:33:00,569
Uh, Sergeant Powell...

595
00:33:01,546 --> 00:33:04,659
Sergeant Deko, and...

596
00:33:04,683 --> 00:33:06,709
Officer Evans over there.

597
00:33:07,919 --> 00:33:10,932
They made me a deal.

598
00:33:10,956 --> 00:33:13,687
Let's go, Mr. Brown.

599
00:33:20,398 --> 00:33:21,642
Well, come on in, Ben.

600
00:33:21,666 --> 00:33:23,811
Meet Marvin Latham,

601
00:33:23,835 --> 00:33:26,066
otherwise known as Al Brown.

602
00:33:27,539 --> 00:33:29,150
I'm Ben Matlock,
defense attorney

603
00:33:29,174 --> 00:33:30,685
in the Marvin Shea case.

604
00:33:30,709 --> 00:33:31,819
Pleasure to meet you.

605
00:33:31,843 --> 00:33:34,355
Likewise, I'm sure.

606
00:33:34,379 --> 00:33:36,591
Is, uh, is your lawyer here?

607
00:33:36,615 --> 00:33:37,592
No.

608
00:33:37,616 --> 00:33:40,061
No, the fact is, I'm doing
just fine without one.

609
00:33:40,085 --> 00:33:42,997
He's agreed to talk about the
Marvin Shea case in exchange

610
00:33:43,021 --> 00:33:45,466
for our dropping one
charge of arson against him.

611
00:33:45,490 --> 00:33:47,435
- Is it all right if I...?
- No, no.

612
00:33:47,459 --> 00:33:49,670
Please, be my guest.

613
00:33:49,694 --> 00:33:53,374
Um, I understand that
Marvin Shea arranged for you

614
00:33:53,398 --> 00:33:55,443
to burn down his clothing store.

615
00:33:55,467 --> 00:33:56,511
That's right.

616
00:33:56,535 --> 00:33:58,513
And, uh, you arranged for him

617
00:33:58,537 --> 00:34:01,449
to meet you in the alley in
back of the Kingston Hotel

618
00:34:01,473 --> 00:34:02,984
to get your down payment.

619
00:34:03,008 --> 00:34:04,151
That's right.

620
00:34:04,175 --> 00:34:05,386
And you met him there

621
00:34:05,410 --> 00:34:07,955
and, uh, found that he was,
uh, backing out of the deal,

622
00:34:07,979 --> 00:34:09,957
and argued with him
and stabbed him to death.

623
00:34:09,981 --> 00:34:11,158
Oh, no, no, no.

624
00:34:11,182 --> 00:34:12,226
Now, that's not right.

625
00:34:12,250 --> 00:34:13,327
It's not?

626
00:34:13,351 --> 00:34:14,495
No.

627
00:34:14,519 --> 00:34:15,930
I never made the meeting.

628
00:34:15,954 --> 00:34:17,331
Oh, come on.

629
00:34:17,355 --> 00:34:19,867
I had flown to
Chicago the day before.

630
00:34:19,891 --> 00:34:21,202
On business.

631
00:34:21,226 --> 00:34:22,970
Furniture store
out in Lincolnwood

632
00:34:22,994 --> 00:34:24,105
went up in flames that night.

633
00:34:24,129 --> 00:34:25,172
Really?

634
00:34:25,196 --> 00:34:27,275
Boy.

635
00:34:27,299 --> 00:34:29,677
That is some
coincidence, isn't it?

636
00:34:29,701 --> 00:34:31,746
Anyway, they had, uh,

637
00:34:31,770 --> 00:34:33,748
closed the airport
because of foul weather

638
00:34:33,772 --> 00:34:36,350
for four hours the next day.

639
00:34:36,374 --> 00:34:38,419
Really screwed
up all the flights.

640
00:34:38,443 --> 00:34:40,388
Just about the time
that I was supposed

641
00:34:40,412 --> 00:34:43,491
to be meeting Mr. Marvin
Shea in that alleyway,

642
00:34:43,515 --> 00:34:46,427
I was actually flying
over Tennessee.

643
00:34:46,451 --> 00:34:48,195
That's a lie.

644
00:34:48,219 --> 00:34:51,732
You were seen arguing with
him, and you were seen killing him.

645
00:34:51,756 --> 00:34:54,936
I'm sorry.

646
00:34:54,960 --> 00:34:56,270
Well, show him.

647
00:34:56,294 --> 00:34:58,306
We found this in his pocket.

648
00:34:58,330 --> 00:34:59,974
It's a ticket stub.

649
00:34:59,998 --> 00:35:02,276
His flight to and from Chicago.

650
00:35:02,300 --> 00:35:04,412
He was on the plane, Ben.

651
00:35:04,436 --> 00:35:06,914
I keep very meticulous records.

652
00:35:06,938 --> 00:35:09,283
You know, for tax purposes.

653
00:35:09,307 --> 00:35:11,786
Ben, your witness lied.

654
00:35:11,810 --> 00:35:13,654
He lied, and I traded off

655
00:35:13,678 --> 00:35:16,557
an arson charge for nothing.

656
00:35:26,057 --> 00:35:27,201
<i>No!</i>

657
00:35:27,225 --> 00:35:29,503
I didn't lie to protect myself.

658
00:35:29,527 --> 00:35:31,505
Look, I was trying to help Sid.

659
00:35:31,529 --> 00:35:33,240
By committing perjury?

660
00:35:34,966 --> 00:35:37,378
Sid is a good guy.

661
00:35:37,402 --> 00:35:39,413
And Marv was a creep.

662
00:35:39,437 --> 00:35:41,282
It isn't right that
Sid should suffer

663
00:35:41,306 --> 00:35:43,969
just because Marv finally
got what he deserved.

664
00:35:51,082 --> 00:35:55,019
Ross... I want you
to tell me something.

665
00:35:56,721 --> 00:35:58,747
Did you do any
drinking that night?

666
00:35:59,824 --> 00:36:02,269
I never had a drop.

667
00:36:02,293 --> 00:36:04,739
Well, I quit on August 25, 1988,

668
00:36:04,763 --> 00:36:06,925
and I haven't had a drop since.

669
00:36:16,207 --> 00:36:17,402
Ben...?

670
00:36:19,110 --> 00:36:20,544
Ben...!

671
00:36:39,330 --> 00:36:40,474
Mr. Matlock.

672
00:36:40,498 --> 00:36:41,575
Oh, hello, Cheryl.

673
00:36:41,599 --> 00:36:42,877
I was just in here

674
00:36:42,901 --> 00:36:46,047
poking around to
see what I could find.

675
00:36:46,071 --> 00:36:49,684
Oh, uh, you must be Mrs. Shea.

676
00:36:49,708 --> 00:36:51,218
My condolences.

677
00:36:51,242 --> 00:36:54,588
I'm Ben Matlock,
uh, Sid's lawyer.

678
00:36:54,612 --> 00:36:55,890
How do you do?

679
00:36:55,914 --> 00:36:57,725
She came to pick
up Marv's things.

680
00:36:57,749 --> 00:37:00,027
Oh, is there anything
I can do to help you?

681
00:37:00,051 --> 00:37:01,362
No.

682
00:37:01,386 --> 00:37:03,330
Oh, are these the
wedding pictures?

683
00:37:03,354 --> 00:37:04,598
I got them yesterday.

684
00:37:04,622 --> 00:37:06,967
I thought you might
like to see them.

685
00:37:06,991 --> 00:37:10,337
Oh, your daughter
looks so beautiful.

686
00:37:10,361 --> 00:37:14,008
And Marv... never
looked so happy.

687
00:37:14,032 --> 00:37:16,811
Look, he's even got
his arm around Ross.

688
00:37:16,835 --> 00:37:18,012
Uh... uh, c-could...

689
00:37:18,036 --> 00:37:20,815
could-could I just look
at those for a minute?

690
00:37:20,839 --> 00:37:22,016
Sure.

691
00:37:22,040 --> 00:37:23,084
Why not?

692
00:37:23,108 --> 00:37:24,351
Thank you.

693
00:37:24,375 --> 00:37:27,655
I love to look at
we-wedding pictures.

694
00:37:27,679 --> 00:37:32,426
Oh, they're having...
some swell time.

695
00:37:43,294 --> 00:37:46,173
Well...

696
00:37:46,197 --> 00:37:49,677
I recalled you to the
stand, Mr. Gordon,

697
00:37:49,701 --> 00:37:53,080
because... the other day,

698
00:37:53,104 --> 00:37:56,871
you didn't exactly tell
the whole truth, did you?

699
00:37:58,343 --> 00:37:59,820
No, sir.

700
00:37:59,844 --> 00:38:03,290
Yeah. Well, now, if you
can tell the whole truth today,

701
00:38:03,314 --> 00:38:06,227
maybe... just-just maybe...
The district attorney

702
00:38:06,251 --> 00:38:08,763
won't file perjury
charges against you.

703
00:38:08,787 --> 00:38:09,997
You think you can

704
00:38:10,021 --> 00:38:12,366
- tell the truth today?
- Yes, sir, I can.

705
00:38:12,390 --> 00:38:13,768
Good, good.

706
00:38:13,792 --> 00:38:16,370
Now, uh, before we...

707
00:38:16,394 --> 00:38:19,373
before we start talking
about, uh, what happened

708
00:38:19,397 --> 00:38:22,276
the night Marvin
Shea was killed,

709
00:38:22,300 --> 00:38:24,545
let's talk about what happened

710
00:38:24,569 --> 00:38:29,030
the night of August 24, 1988.

711
00:38:30,375 --> 00:38:33,187
I'm... afraid you lost me.

712
00:38:33,211 --> 00:38:38,659
Well... the night
of August 24, 1988,

713
00:38:38,683 --> 00:38:42,596
a man and woman were killed

714
00:38:42,620 --> 00:38:46,233
in an automobile accident
out on the Old Ridge Road.

715
00:38:46,257 --> 00:38:50,204
A hit-and-run driver
forced their car off the road

716
00:38:50,228 --> 00:38:52,706
and into a tree, and witnesses

717
00:38:52,730 --> 00:38:55,609
at the scene say
that hit-and-run driver

718
00:38:55,633 --> 00:38:57,124
had to be drunk.

719
00:38:58,703 --> 00:39:01,081
I-I'm sorry, I don't know
what you're talking about.

720
00:39:01,105 --> 00:39:03,317
Of course you do.

721
00:39:03,341 --> 00:39:05,653
And if Marvin Shea were here,

722
00:39:05,677 --> 00:39:08,010
he'd know, too, wouldn't he?

723
00:39:09,247 --> 00:39:13,627
I found this hidden
away, way in the back

724
00:39:13,651 --> 00:39:16,730
of one of the
drawers in his desk.

725
00:39:16,754 --> 00:39:18,382
See?

726
00:39:19,724 --> 00:39:22,403
You didn't know he
had that, did you?

727
00:39:22,427 --> 00:39:24,405
See that?

728
00:39:24,429 --> 00:39:26,907
Hit-and-run.

729
00:39:26,931 --> 00:39:31,545
He kept this... because...

730
00:39:31,569 --> 00:39:35,649
he knew that drunk
driver was you, didn't he?

731
00:39:35,673 --> 00:39:37,318
That's not true.

732
00:39:37,342 --> 00:39:38,708
Mm-hmm.

733
00:39:40,311 --> 00:39:43,224
You used to drink
quite a lot, didn't you?

734
00:39:43,248 --> 00:39:45,426
I don't drink.

735
00:39:45,450 --> 00:39:47,361
Yeah, but years
ago, you used to...

736
00:39:47,385 --> 00:39:51,265
you used to put it down
pretty good, didn't you?

737
00:39:51,289 --> 00:39:53,190
Yes.

738
00:39:55,326 --> 00:39:57,338
When did you get sober?

739
00:39:57,362 --> 00:39:58,706
Close to three years.

740
00:39:58,730 --> 00:40:03,811
You got sober August 25, 1988,

741
00:40:03,835 --> 00:40:05,779
the day after the accident.

742
00:40:05,803 --> 00:40:07,448
And you got sober

743
00:40:07,472 --> 00:40:11,518
because you knew your drinking
had killed two innocent people.

744
00:40:11,542 --> 00:40:12,753
No!

745
00:40:12,777 --> 00:40:14,021
Witnesses...

746
00:40:14,045 --> 00:40:17,591
say that that hit-and-run
car was dark in color,

747
00:40:17,615 --> 00:40:18,826
four-door sedan.

748
00:40:18,850 --> 00:40:21,295
You used to drive a
car like that, didn't you?

749
00:40:21,319 --> 00:40:23,464
- Well, yes.
- And you sold that

750
00:40:23,488 --> 00:40:27,468
car two and a half weeks
after the accident, didn't you?

751
00:40:27,492 --> 00:40:29,136
I-I don't remember.

752
00:40:29,160 --> 00:40:30,304
Uh-huh.

753
00:40:30,328 --> 00:40:33,040
Where did you take
the car to get it repaired?

754
00:40:33,064 --> 00:40:35,709
Your Honor, we fail to
see the relevancy of this

755
00:40:35,733 --> 00:40:37,493
- line of questioning.
- Relevance?!

756
00:40:39,470 --> 00:40:43,050
I found where he took
the car to have it repaired,

757
00:40:43,074 --> 00:40:45,986
and traces of green
paint were found

758
00:40:46,010 --> 00:40:49,056
on the right-hand
side of that car, and the

759
00:40:49,080 --> 00:40:51,959
car that was run off
the road was green.

760
00:40:51,983 --> 00:40:53,627
Overruled. Mr. Matlock,

761
00:40:53,651 --> 00:40:55,745
- please get on with it.
- Yes, sir.

762
00:40:57,121 --> 00:40:59,700
Somehow...

763
00:40:59,724 --> 00:41:04,838
Marvin Shea knew you were
that drunk hit-and-run driver,

764
00:41:04,862 --> 00:41:08,108
but he didn't say anything;
he kept quiet about it.

765
00:41:08,132 --> 00:41:11,045
And you didn't go to prison...

766
00:41:11,069 --> 00:41:13,247
As long as you kept up...

767
00:41:13,271 --> 00:41:16,216
a little arrangement.

768
00:41:16,240 --> 00:41:17,785
Like, uh...

769
00:41:17,809 --> 00:41:20,955
taking home... half commissions.

770
00:41:20,979 --> 00:41:22,656
No, that's not true.

771
00:41:22,680 --> 00:41:25,025
He worked you
unreasonable hours,

772
00:41:25,049 --> 00:41:27,494
and you took home
less than half the pay

773
00:41:27,518 --> 00:41:29,863
the other salesmen took home.

774
00:41:29,887 --> 00:41:32,766
And you even worked at
his house on weekends.

775
00:41:32,790 --> 00:41:36,770
But you did it because you
knew that, at any moment,

776
00:41:36,794 --> 00:41:40,007
he could pull the
string and destroy you.

777
00:41:40,031 --> 00:41:42,543
So you put up with his
insults, his cheapness,

778
00:41:42,567 --> 00:41:45,145
the horrible way he treated you.

779
00:41:45,169 --> 00:41:47,081
Well, you got tired of it,

780
00:41:47,105 --> 00:41:49,984
found an opportunity,
and killed him.

781
00:41:50,008 --> 00:41:51,418
No!

782
00:41:51,442 --> 00:41:53,274
Yes, you did.

783
00:41:56,547 --> 00:41:59,176
These are copies...
Here you go...

784
00:42:01,219 --> 00:42:04,417
of some photographs
that were taken...

785
00:42:06,557 --> 00:42:09,470
at the party the
night he was killed.

786
00:42:09,494 --> 00:42:12,506
In this first one, he
was still alive, see?

787
00:42:12,530 --> 00:42:14,842
There's you next to
him and his daughter

788
00:42:14,866 --> 00:42:17,911
and the rest of the
people from the store.

789
00:42:17,935 --> 00:42:20,481
This one was taken
after he was killed.

790
00:42:20,505 --> 00:42:25,386
Uh, it's hard to see,
but that clock says 9:50.

791
00:42:25,410 --> 00:42:26,854
Can you see that? Yeah.

792
00:42:26,878 --> 00:42:28,055
The security guard

793
00:42:28,079 --> 00:42:32,192
who found the body
called the police at 9:40.

794
00:42:32,216 --> 00:42:34,428
That means this picture
had to have been made

795
00:42:34,452 --> 00:42:37,598
just moments before
the police arrived.

796
00:42:37,622 --> 00:42:39,767
- I suppose so.
- Yeah.

797
00:42:39,791 --> 00:42:41,919
The odd thing is...

798
00:42:43,594 --> 00:42:47,822
you're wearing...
two different ties.

799
00:42:51,035 --> 00:42:53,698
Look at this. Look at this.

800
00:42:55,039 --> 00:42:57,351
He's wearing two different ties.

801
00:42:57,375 --> 00:42:59,686
You changed ties in
the middle of the party.

802
00:42:59,710 --> 00:43:01,076
How come?

803
00:43:03,748 --> 00:43:07,094
And what-what's even odder...

804
00:43:07,118 --> 00:43:10,697
is this tie that you're
wearing in this second picture

805
00:43:10,721 --> 00:43:15,402
is exactly like the
tie Marv was wearing.

806
00:43:15,426 --> 00:43:16,503
You see that?

807
00:43:16,527 --> 00:43:18,587
How do you explain that?

808
00:43:21,833 --> 00:43:26,080
Well... I went out and rented...

809
00:43:26,104 --> 00:43:28,982
two tuxedo ties...

810
00:43:29,006 --> 00:43:32,486
exactly like the
ones in that picture.

811
00:43:32,510 --> 00:43:34,154
See that?

812
00:43:34,178 --> 00:43:35,989
And-And you know
something about clothes...

813
00:43:36,013 --> 00:43:39,359
They're easy to take off
and put on, aren't they?

814
00:43:39,383 --> 00:43:42,611
- Yeah.
- Well... how about this?

815
00:43:44,222 --> 00:43:48,268
You left the party
shortly before Marv did,

816
00:43:48,292 --> 00:43:50,537
waited for him...

817
00:43:50,561 --> 00:43:53,607
and when he walked
out into that alley

818
00:43:53,631 --> 00:43:56,143
you jumped up and stabbed him!

819
00:43:56,167 --> 00:43:58,312
And he grabbed
that knife in his chest,

820
00:43:58,336 --> 00:44:00,881
and then he grabbed
at you! That's right!

821
00:44:00,905 --> 00:44:03,317
And all he could
touch was your tie.

822
00:44:03,341 --> 00:44:05,486
Run!

823
00:44:05,510 --> 00:44:08,422
Run! And that's what
you would've done, run.

824
00:44:08,446 --> 00:44:10,991
But you realized...

825
00:44:11,015 --> 00:44:12,793
he had blood on his hands,

826
00:44:12,817 --> 00:44:16,686
and there'd be
blood on your tie.

827
00:44:18,823 --> 00:44:21,156
So that's why...

828
00:44:24,462 --> 00:44:26,874
when you came
back into that party...

829
00:44:28,199 --> 00:44:30,191
you were wearing Marv's tie.

830
00:44:33,571 --> 00:44:37,064
And I have one more
photograph that proves it.

831
00:44:38,342 --> 00:44:44,258
The coroner's
photograph... of Marv.

832
00:44:44,282 --> 00:44:45,626
See?

833
00:44:45,650 --> 00:44:50,297
He's wearing the
tie you were wearing

834
00:44:50,321 --> 00:44:52,299
in that first picture.

835
00:44:52,323 --> 00:44:55,469
See, I remembered
this coroner's picture,

836
00:44:55,493 --> 00:44:58,372
and I remembered
the blood on the tie,

837
00:44:58,396 --> 00:44:59,907
and I remembered

838
00:44:59,931 --> 00:45:03,810
these pictures of you,
and it really wasn't hard

839
00:45:03,834 --> 00:45:06,613
to figure out...

840
00:45:06,637 --> 00:45:09,869
how the blood got on that tie.

841
00:45:17,782 --> 00:45:20,627
We find the
defendant not guilty.

842
00:45:31,929 --> 00:45:34,074
Hey, Ben! You made it!

843
00:45:34,098 --> 00:45:36,410
Business is
certainly picking up!

844
00:45:36,434 --> 00:45:38,378
Oh, it's been this way
ever since the trial.

845
00:45:38,402 --> 00:45:39,813
I guess it was
all that publicity.

846
00:45:40,838 --> 00:45:42,349
Come over here; I want
to show you something.

847
00:45:42,373 --> 00:45:43,350
Huh?

848
00:45:43,374 --> 00:45:45,652
Our new salesman's
got lots of great ideas.

849
00:45:45,676 --> 00:45:47,354
He wants to update our lines

850
00:45:47,378 --> 00:45:50,691
and stop carrying the
slower-moving suits.

851
00:45:50,715 --> 00:45:54,027
Uh... you-you don't
mean... I'm afraid so.

852
00:45:54,051 --> 00:45:56,296
But this is the only
place I can get 'em,

853
00:45:56,320 --> 00:45:57,931
and if I have to have
'em custom-made,

854
00:45:57,955 --> 00:46:00,968
it'll cost me an arm and a
leg... What am I gonna do?

855
00:46:00,992 --> 00:46:04,861
You're gonna have to
stock up, that's what.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
